Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Red Sample Nine Stops the Kick
- Story & Context: During the first curing set, Akiko selects lacquer sample card number nine and uses it to define the opening rule: every ninth strong biwa attack must erase the next programmed kick cluster completely. Malik begins with a sparse footwork pattern, but as the track develops the listener starts anticipating the missing burst as strongly as the audible ones.

Ren assigns one deadened vibraphone bar to each erased kick, producing a short dry metallic answer in the empty space. Tessa remains silent until the fourth deletion, then enters with a cracked low bass-oboe note just before the electronic sub returns. The middle section increases kick density while keeping the same ninth-attack rule, making each silence feel increasingly violent.

The final cycle removes not only the kick cluster but every drum layer for one full bar. Akiko plays a single hard downward biwa stroke into the empty room, and the groove resumes two beats later from an off-center kick.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Vermilion lacquer sample card marked 9 on a low black worktable, large biwa plectrum beside it, one muted drum-machine step glowing faintly, prepared vibraphone bar and bass-oboe keywork in soft focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to "nine cuts," "leave the kick," "one bar empty," and "resume late."

Track 2: Chain on the Third Bar
- Story & Context: Ren lays a short light chain across the third vibraphone bar and discovers that its attack produces a bright metallic buzz followed by an uneven decay. Malik assigns the sound to a position normally occupied by a clap but leaves enough space for the chain to remain physically audible.

Akiko responds with two-note biwa figures that end before the prepared bar rings. Tessa answers every third chain strike with a short falling bass-oboe interval while the electronic sub stays silent during those responses. Midway through, Ren removes the chain. The same vibraphone note suddenly becomes clean and stable, which makes the track feel strangely less rhythmic despite the unchanged drum pattern.

After sixteen bars, the chain returns and the groove regains its rough edge. The track ends with Ren stopping the bar by hand while the last sub note continues alone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro documentary photograph of a short metal chain resting across the third vibraphone bar, felt strips on adjacent bars, biwa strings crossing the lower edge of frame, bass-oboe reed and lacquer brush nearby. Sparse spoken fragments limited to "third bar," "leave the chain," "clean once," and "stop it by hand."

Track 3: Courtyard Air After the Last Burst
- Story & Context: The EP closer is built around the narrow courtyard beside the workshop. The door remains closed during the first half, keeping the room acoustically dry while Malik runs the densest footwork pattern on the record. Akiko uses shorter and more frequent biwa strikes, Ren alternates deadened and ringing vibraphone bars, and Tessa contributes only breath noise and key clicks.

After the final planned kick burst, the drums stop abruptly and the courtyard door is opened. The room tone changes immediately: distant bicycle tires, a small drainage channel, and cool exterior air enter the microphones. Akiko responds with the longest sustained biwa resonance of the EP while Ren allows one unprepared vibraphone bar to ring freely.

Malik does not restore the full groove. He brings back only a sparse kick every other beat and one short sub pulse after each bass-oboe note. The EP ends with the door sliding closed and one wooden drawer clicking shut in the newly dry room.
